 "beats": [
  {
   "say": "Righto mate, a binary tree is just a hierarchy of nodes, with one starting point up the top called the root. Think of it like the first bloke in the family tree, no dramas.",
   "reveal": [
    "title",
    "root"
   ],
   "emphasize": [
    "root"
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "From that root, you can branch down to a left child and a right child. Those labels matter, because binary trees keep the two sides distinct, which is pretty bloody handy.",
   "reveal": [
    "leftChild",
    "rightChild",
    "arrowLeft",
    "arrowRight"
   ],
   "emphasize": [
    "arrowLeft",
    "arrowRight"
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "The key shape is top-down: parents sit above, and their children sit below. So the structure tells us who belongs under who, nice and clean, not some dodgy pile of data.",
   "reveal": [
    "hierarchyNote"
   ],
   "emphasize": [
    "root",
    "leftChild",
    "rightChild",
    "hierarchyNote"
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "And here's the big rule, mate: binary means each node can have at most two children. It can have zero, one, or two, but never three — that's the whole bloody point.",
